<VI _name="lvbd_remove_bad_wires.vi" description="Clears the BROKEN wires a scripted edit left on a VI's block diagram - nested diagrams included - and says exactly what went. The shared last step of lvai_swap_subvis\2C lvai_bind_typedef_constants and lvai_add_class_method. Based on lvbd_broken_wires\2C which finds them\3B this one also removes them.\0A\0AIN THIS ORDER\3A (1) traverse every Wire and keep the broken ones. With NONE the VI is neither changed nor saved. (2) For each broken wire record its END TERMINALS - name and owner class\2C sources apart from sinks - then run BD.Remove Bad Wires and Save.Instrument. (3) Read back every recorded SINK (Is Source? false) of a broken wire that HAD a source\3A a sink that carries no wire afterwards is a connection the cleanup took away\2C and it is listed in Inputs Left Unwired. A wire with no source end is a loose fragment - clearing it loses nothing\2C so its sinks are not checked.\0A\0AEach Broken Wires line is the sources\2C an arrow\2C then the sinks - every end written as name @ owner class followed by a semicolon. Connected Wire is read with its OWN error path per terminal\3B a read that errors lands in Inputs Unchecked rather than in Inputs Left Unwired\2C because a failed read is not evidence of a lost wire.\0A\0AOpened in the IDE application instance through Application\3AProject\3AActive Project\2C so it edits the copy the project holds - Error 1055 means no project was active. UNMEASURED as a whole\3A Traverse for Wire\2C Is Broken? and Terminals[] are lvbd_broken_wires' and measured\3B Remove Bad Wires is measured in lvbd_graft_rewire\3B reading Connected Wire on a terminal after Remove Bad Wires is not.">
